  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...\nIssued 326 AM MST MON NOV 9 2026\nMild southerly flow will continue on Thursday and Friday as a \nhighly amplified ridge remains centered to our east. An initial \nupper level trough will split as it approaches the coast, but a\ndeeper low is then expected to move inland late Friday into the\nweekend. Temperatures will be near record highs through late \nweek with dry and breezy to windy conditions. Gusts 35-50 mph \nare possible on Friday across the open terrain of southeast \nOregon with gusts 20-40 mph elsewhere.\n\nPrecipitation may arrive as early as Friday night as a shortwave\ntrough rotates inland, but uncertainty remains in how far south\nthis system will reach. A 20-50% chance of precipitation will \ndevelop Friday night and Saturday across east-central Oregon and\nthe southwest Idaho mountains, with precipitation chances in \nthe higher terrain aided by moist southwest flow aloft.\nPrecipitation chances will remain less than 20% elsewhere. High\nsnow levels around 6500-7500 feet will limit snow accumulation \nto the peaks. Another shortwave trough will push inland Sunday \nand Monday, increasing the chance of precipitation to 30-60% in \nall areas with the highest chances in the mountains. Snow levels\nshould remain high through Sunday, then lower by Monday to \n4000-5500 feet as colder air arrives with the trough. This \nshould also cool high temperatures to near normal on Monday. \nWinds will be gusty through early Sunday, then taper off \nslightly late Sunday and Monday as the trough moves through.",
